Everything the course hands you, in one place. Each of these is introduced in the session that first needs it — this page is where to find it again in week nine, when you need the persona template and can't remember which session it came from.

Nothing here is busywork reference material. Every item is something you are expected to actually use on an artifact you are graded on.

**Start with the <course-link type="page" id="pg-rubrics">Assignment Rubrics</course-link>.** Every checkpoint is graded on a published four-level grid, and reading the grid before you start is most of the work of satisfying it. It is listed again under *Evaluation instruments* below.

---

## Discovery — talking to real people without fooling yourself

The hardest part of founding a project is finding out whether the problem is real. These are the tools for that, and they matter most in Weeks 2–3 and again any time your evidence starts looking thin.

- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-mom-test-summary">The Mom Test — Key Ideas</course-link>** — the rules for talking to users without leading them into telling you what you want to hear. Read this before your first interview.
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-good-vs-leading-questions">Good vs. Leading Questions</course-link>** — side-by-side rewrites of the questions that produce useless answers. The fastest way to see the difference in your own draft questions.
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-interview-prompt-cards">Interview Prompt Cards</course-link>** — questions to carry into a real conversation, so you are not composing them under pressure.
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-interview-ethics">Interview Ethics for Students</course-link>** — consent, privacy, and where a class project sits relative to BYU's IRB. **Read this before you talk to anyone.**
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-discovery-notes">Discovery Notes</course-link>** — what to write down while you are still in the conversation. These become the evidence in your proposal.
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-persona-template">Persona Template</course-link>** — for building an honest, evidence-tagged proxy when a real user is genuinely out of reach.
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-persona-examples">Persona Examples</course-link>** — the same persona written from evidence and written from imagination, so you can tell which one you just wrote.

---

## Scoping and feasibility — what is worth building, and what you can actually finish

Founding projects fail far more often on scope than on skill. These instruments force the arithmetic before you commit to it.

- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-idea-scoring-sheet">Idea Scoring Sheet</course-link>** — score your candidate ideas against a shared yardstick instead of against how excited you feel today.
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-feasibility-risk-worksheet">Feasibility &amp; Risk Worksheet</course-link>** — the six risk categories and the know / learn / avoid triage.
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-learning-plan-template">Learning Plan Template</course-link>** — naming what you will have to learn, and how you will know you have learned it.
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-spikes-reading">Spikes</course-link>** — the time-boxed experiment that de-risks an unknown before you build on top of it. At least one spike is required, and it is graded in your proposal.
- **[Creative Problem Solving handbook (PDF)]({{ CPS_HANDBOOK_URL }})** — the divergent/convergent spine the whole course runs on. Hosted externally; the sessions that assign it name the pages.

---

## Writing and proposals — arguing for a project to someone who owes you nothing

- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-proposal-anatomy-reading">The Anatomy of a Software Proposal</course-link>** — nine sections, what each one is *for*, how each fails, and the order to write them in (which is not the order they appear in).
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-prd-reference">PRD Reference</course-link>** — what a product requirements document contains, and what it is not.
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-sample-proposal-weak">Sample Proposal — CostumeHub (weak)</course-link>** — read it and mark every place you stop believing the author.
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-sample-proposal-strong">Sample Proposal — CostumeHub (strong)</course-link>** — the same project, argued well. The difference is discovery and cutting, not writing talent.

*Both samples are teaching samples: the interviews, counts, and numbers in them are invented and labeled as such. Don't cite them as real evidence.*

---

## Open source — the decisions that let other people join you

- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-license-cheat-sheet">Open-Source License Cheat Sheet</course-link>** — permissive vs. copyleft, and what each choice commits you to. You defend your licensing decision in your Infrastructure Package.
- **<course-link type="page" id="pg-starting-your-repository">Starting Your Project Repository</course-link>** — the organization, the public repository, the first commits, and what must never go in one. Done at your team's first meeting.

